course: dictionary
lesson: 10773
title: Vacation or holiday?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are different in different countries but mean the same thing. In America and Canada, a family «goes on vacation» in the summer. In Britain, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, the same family goes on «holiday». Both words mean a time away from school or work, often for fun.
explain: Use the words around the word to work out its meaning. If a story says «The school closed for the summer vacation», the clues tell you it is the long break when school is shut. In American English, vacation can also be a verb: «They vacation in Florida every winter» means they spend their holiday there.
explain: Being different does not make a word wrong. A child in London and a child in Chicago are talking about the same kind of break. When you read a book from another country, check the context, then put your own word into the sentence to see if it still fits.
fact: Vacation is the American and Canadian word for a holiday: a time away from school or work.
fact: In America, the summer vacation is the long break when schools are closed.
fact: In British English, the same break is usually called the holidays. Vacation is used for the time when universities and law courts are closed.
fact: Vacation can also be a verb in American English: «They vacation in Florida» means they spend a holiday there.
fact: Other British and American pairs: «lift» and «elevator», «torch» and «flashlight», «petrol» and «gas», «flat» and «apartment».
exercise: «We are going on vacation to Florida next month.» What does vacation mean here? | Something to eat on a plane | A big, dark cave | *A holiday away from home or school | A kind of bus
exercise: Lily from London reads, «The school vacation starts on Friday.» What would a British child say instead? | Queue | *Holiday | Flat | Lift
exercise: Which British word matches the American word «elevator»? | Torch | Queue | *Lift | Flat
test: «They vacation in Florida every winter.» What does vacation mean as a verb here? | To go to school | To play football | *To spend a holiday somewhere | To clean the house
test: Which British word matches the American word «flashlight»? | Queue | Flat | *Torch | Petrol
exam: Which pair is a British word and its American partner? | Dog and cat | *Queue and line | Happy and sad | Run and jump
